  "boxes": [
    {
      "type": "anchorbox",
      "title": "The Dedicated Executive",
      "description": "Hanuman combines deep intellectual training (Bidyavan) and virtue (Guni) with an intense eagerness to execute Lord Rama's work. In the *Valmiki Ramayana* (*Kishkindha Kanda, 44:11*), Rama praises Hanuman by stating that in him alone reside strength, intellect, prowess, adaptability to time and place, and moral guidance.",
      "motivating_text": "",
      "citation": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "gotchabox",
      "items": [
        "The \"Word-Initial\" vs \"Suffix\" Rule (बिद्यावान): Why बिद्यावान and not बिद्याबान? Because the first v carries the heavy front door weight of the word, it requires maximum articulatory force and hardens into the plosive b. The v in -vaan is a possession suffix (वतुँप् प्रत्यय). Because it occurs at the end of the word where the mouth naturally relaxes (Mukha-Sukha / Lenition), it stays as the gliding breathy v."
      ]
    }
  ]
